; Deliberately empty of settings. Read this before adding one.
;
; This file used to contain `legacy-peer-deps=true`, and it arrived by
; inheritance rather than by decision: commit 768d2b7 stood this repo up as a
; standalone extraction from Vox Pop and brought the file along. Over there it
; is defensible — it was added by a React Native commit (d214b2f2), and the RN
; ecosystem's peer ranges genuinely do conflict. None of that applies here.
;
; What it cost: `legacy-peer-deps` makes npm ignore peerDependencies ENTIRELY.
; Not "install them loosely" — ignore. A conflicting peer produces no warning at
; all, and `--strict-peer-deps` on the command line does not override the
; setting. Both were verified; the second is the surprising one.
;
; That matters here specifically because `@antiphony/shared` declares `zod` as a
; peer (see its package.json note). The peer declaration is the mechanism that
; tells a CONSUMER's npm the contract needs one shared zod instance, and this
; setting is how a repo ends up unable to see that kind of conflict in its own
; tree.
;
; Removing it changed nothing that was already wrong, and that is the point
; rather than a disappointment: `npm install` exits 0 with ZERO ERESOLVE and
; zero peer warnings — the tree resolves its peers cleanly today. The five
; `npm warn` lines it prints are `install-scripts` notices about `allowScripts`
; and have nothing to do with peers. So this removal is free now and pays later,
; when a conflict that would otherwise be silent has somewhere to show up.
;
; ⚠️ Do not read peer checking as protection against a peer range that is simply
; WRONG. #148 is the standing example: `@hono/zod-openapi` declares
; `zod: >=3.0.0` while its own dependency needs `^3.20.2`. Nothing was hidden by
; this setting — zod 3.25.76 SATISFIES `>=3.0.0`, so npm was correct to stay
; quiet, and `/openapi.json` still returned 500 under zod 4. No npm setting
; catches an upstream range that overstates what its code supports.
;
; If you hit a peer conflict you cannot resolve, prefer `overrides` in the root
; package.json for that ONE package. It is narrow, it is visible in a diff, and
; it does not switch off peer resolution for the whole tree.
